Here is an example LATEX command that creates a chessboard with a given fen notation:

\documentclass{article}
\usepackage{tikz}

\begin{document}

% Define the fen notation
\tikzset{
  fen/.style={%
    minimum width=1cm,
    minimum height=1cm
  }
}

% Draw the chessboard
\begin{tikzon}
  \foreach \i in {0,...,8} {
    \foreach \j in {0,\fen{\i},1,...,7} {
      \node at (\j/\fen*\fen+2cm) {};
    }
  }
\end{tikznodes}

\end{document}

This code uses the tikz package to create a chessboard using the fen notation provided by the \tikzon environment. The \foreach loop iterates over the rows and columns of the chess board, drawing each square as a node with a label indicating its position on the board. The \fen command converts the current fen notation into a string representation for use in the tikz environment.

